A three-way extent split re-finds between its two halves

ext4_ext_convert_to_initialized splits an extent twice when the range
being converted sits in the MIDDLE of it: once at the far edge, once at
the near one. Between those two calls the tree can change -- the first
split grows it when the leaf it lands in is already full -- and
ext4_ext_split_extent_at reads (*ppath)[depth].extent directly, without
looking it up. The path handed to the second call could therefore point
into a leaf that no longer holds the block being split, and the second
split then rewrote the wrong extent, leaving an index that disagrees with
its leaves. e2fsck calls the result "invalid extent node".

Nothing reached it until patch 0046. The other two branches split once, and
the pre-existing caller converts from the start of an extent, which is the
left-hand case. ext4_extent_mark_written converts the middle, because that
is what a preallocated write does: macOS reserves the space, the data
arrives a piece at a time, and each piece is marked written where it lands.

Measured with Tests/run_mount_data_tests.sh, which writes a 64 MB file into
preallocated space on a volume with a partial last group and reads it back
cold. Before: 10 of 24 runs corrupted -- invalid extent node, EIO at 48 MB,
and group descriptors claiming more free blocks than they hold. With the
fast path compiled out (EXT4B_NO_UNWRITTEN_FASTPATH=1), 0 of 16, which is
what identified this code as the site.

Two things about measuring it. There is a clean window of roughly six runs
after every extension restart, so a short batch scores zero on any build and
an early attempt at this bisect returned 0/6 on BOTH arms and was worthless.
And a competing explanation -- ext4b_mount tolerating EEXIST from
ext4_device_register, which would bind a second volume to the first one's
block device -- was instrumented rather than argued, and never fired once in
fourteen runs.

Linux performs the same re-find at the same point, and says why: "Update
path is required because previous ext4_split_extent_at() may result in
split of original leaf or extent zeroout."

--- a/src/ext4_extent.c
+++ b/src/ext4_extent.c
@@ -1981,6 +1981,30 @@
 		err = ext4_ext_split_extent_at(inode_ref, ppath, split + blocks,
 					       EXT4_EXT_MARK_UNWRIT1 |
 						   EXT4_EXT_MARK_UNWRIT2);
+		if (err == EOK) {
+			/*
+			 * Re-find before the second split. The first one can
+			 * grow the tree -- a leaf that is already full is split
+			 * to make room -- and ext4_ext_split_extent_at reads
+			 * (*ppath)[depth].extent directly, without looking it
+			 * up. So the path handed to it here may point into a
+			 * leaf that no longer holds `split`, and the second
+			 * split then rewrites the wrong extent, leaving a tree
+			 * whose index no longer agrees with its leaves.
+			 *
+			 * Only this three-way case is exposed, because only it
+			 * splits twice; and only a caller converting the MIDDLE
+			 * of an unwritten extent reaches it, which is what
+			 * ext4_extent_mark_written() (patch 0046) does for every
+			 * preallocated write. e2fsck calls the result "invalid
+			 * extent node", and a 64 MB file copied into
+			 * preallocated space then reads back EIO.
+			 *
+			 * Linux does the same re-find at the same point, for the
+			 * same stated reason.
+			 */
+			err = ext4_find_extent(inode_ref, split, ppath, 0);
+		}
 		if (err == EOK) {
 			err = ext4_ext_split_extent_at(inode_ref, ppath, split,
 						       EXT4_EXT_MARK_UNWRIT1);
